 per-request latencies, split into overall/GET/SCAN slices; aggregator.totalRecv reports sample counts.\"\n          ]\n        },\n        {\n          \"name\": \"Main workload loop\",\n          \"lines\": \"cmd/workload_client/main.go:67-307\",\n          \"details\": [\n            \"Parses flags (server, duration, rate, get-frac, scan-limit, key prefix/space, log path, send workers), validates arguments, seeds RNGs per worker.\",\n            \"Creates a shared rate limiter (golang.org/x/time/rate), spawns send goroutines that build payloads (buildPayload, line 363) and assign reqIDs (makeReqID, line 308).\",\n            \"Spawns recv goroutines with deadlines that parse responses, extract reqIDs (parseReqID, line 320), compute latency, and push records into latencyCh.\",\n            \"Aggregator + monitor goroutines track outstanding requests and total received counts.\"\n          ]\n        },\n        {\n          \"name\": \"Statistics reporting\",\n          \"lines\": \"cmd/workload_client/main.go:308-451\",\n          \"details\": [\n            \"Helper functions (makeReqID/parseReqID/writeHex/parseHex) ensure both sides share the encoding.\",\n            \"percentile/average/reportLatencyStats (lines 376-451) sort copies of the latency slices, compute p50/p90/p99/avg, and print textual summaries that run.sh’s regex parser expects.\"\n          ]\n        }\n      ],\n      \"cross_dependencies\": [\n        \"run.sh’s run_client wrapper invokes this with -log pointing at logs/client.log; workload/run_client.sh uses go run for convenience.\",\n        \"Server assumes GET payload format 'GET key [reqID]' and SCAN 'SCAN start limit [reqID]' as implemented here.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    \"internal/server\": {\n      \"high_level_role\": \"Core server runtime, including configuration validation, UDP worker pool, request parser, stats logging, and eBPF policy integration.\",\n      \"key_files\": [\n        {\n          \"path\": \"internal/server/config.go\",\n          \"summary\": \"Config struct + Validate clamp runtime knobs to sane defaults so CLI/script errors don’t crash the server.\",\n          \"key_functions\": [\n            {\n              \"name\": \"Config.Validate\",\n              \"lines\": \"internal/server/config.go:19-43\",\n              \"details\": [\n                \"Sets ListenAddr default (127.0.0.1:9000), ensures at least one worker, caps MaxScanKeys >= 1 (default 100k), enforces non-zero timeouts/delays.\",\n                \"Called by server.New before any socket/eBPF work, giving run.sh freedom to pass zero/negative placeholder values.\"\n              ]\n            }\n          ]\n        },\n        {\n          \"path\": \"internal/server/server.go\",\n          \"summary\": \"Implements the Server struct, worker lifecycle, UDP handler, and stats reporter.\",\n          \"key_functions\": [\n            {\n              \"name\": \"Server.New\",\n              \"lines\": \"internal/server/server.go:104-131\",\n              \"details\": [\n                \"Validates config, builds default logger when nil, loads eBPF policy via loadEBPF, clears pinned target map when a program is present, and preallocates workerStats.\"\n              ]\n            },\n            {\n              \"name\": \"Server.Run\",\n              \"lines\": \"internal/server/server.go:144-182\",\n              \"details\": [\n                \"Resets stats, spawns stats logger, iterates workers to open UDP sockets (openWorker, lines 184-230), optionally attaches the eBPF program to worker 0, records socket fds into the pinned map, launches spawnWorker goroutines, and blocks until ctx cancellation.\"\n              ]\n            },\n            {\n              \"name\": \"openWorker + spawnWorker\",\n              \"lines\": \"internal/server/server.go:184-266\",\n              \"details\": [\n                \"openWorker sets SO_REUSEADDR/SO_REUSEPORT via net.ListenConfig.Control and attaches the eBPF program once; returns both UDPConn and fd for map updates.\",\n                \"spawnWorker loops on ReadFromUDP, handles ctx cancellation/ErrClosed gracefully, and logs transient read/write errors.\"\n              ]\n            },\n            {\n              \"name\": \"handleRequest\",\n              \"lines\": \"internal/server/server.go:268-325\",\n              \"details\": [\n                \"Parses payloads via parseRequest, extracts optional reqID tokens, enforces SCAN limit <= MaxScanKeys, executes accurate busy-wait delays for GET/SCAN, records latencies per worker, and formats responses (formatResponse, lines 397-407).\"\n              ]\n            },\n            {\n              \"name\": \"startStatsLogger\",\n              \"lines\": \"internal/server/server.go:328-395\",\n              \"details\": [\n                \"Every second, snapshots worker metrics, logs per-worker averages when there was traffic, accumulates totals, and emits aggregate '[perf] interval=1s ...' lines consumed by observability tooling.\"\n              ]\n            }\n          ]\n        },\n        {\n          \"path\": \"internal/server/handler.go\",\n          \"summary\": \"Robust ASCII parser for GET/SCAN requests.\",\n          \"key_functions\": [\n            {\n              \"name\": \"parseRequest\",\n              \"lines\": \"internal/server/handler.go:14-45\",\n              \"details\": [\n                \"Trims whitespace, splits fields, enforces argument counts for GET/SCAN, and lowercases manually via equalFoldASCII (lines 48-66) to avoid allocations in hot paths.\"\n              ]\n            }\n          ]\n        },\n        {\n          \"path\": \"internal/server/ebpf.go\",\n          \"summary\": \"Loads eBPF selector programs, manages pin directories, and updates reuseport target maps.\",\n          \"key_functions\": [\n            {\n              \"name\": \"loadEBPF + load* variants\",\n              \"lines\": \"internal/server/ebpf.go:50-218\",\n              \"details\": [\n                \"Switches on policy names, ensures memlock rlimit is raised, creates pin directories, loads generated ebpfutil objects, seeds state maps (e.g., PebbleRrState.Active = workers), and returns ebpfPolicy handles with Close hooks.\",\n                \"Validates non-negative worker counts for agent/scan_split/hash policies.\"\n              ]\n            },\n            {\n              \"name\": \"attach/updateTargetsMap/clearTargets\",\n              \"lines\": \"internal/server/ebpf.go:220-268\",\n              \"details\": [\n                \"attach(fd) wraps SO_ATTACH_REUSEPORT_EBPF, updateTargetsMap writes worker fd values into the pinned pebble_udp_targets map so eBPF programs can redirect flows, clearTargets removes stale slots at startup (skips when map not present).\"\n              ]\n            }\n          ]\n        }\n      ],\n      \"cross_dependencies\": [\n        \"Relies on generated internal/ebpfutil bindings (bpf2go outputs).\",\n        \"Server expects run.sh or scripts/launch_server.sh to set BPF_PIN_BASE/PATH correctly.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    \"internal/ebpfutil\": {\n      \"high_level_role\": \"Generated Go bindings/objects for each eBPF selector program; rebuild with `go generate ./internal/ebpfutil`.\",\n      \"key_files\": [\n        {\n          \"path\": \"internal/ebpfutil/bpf_assets.go\",\n          \"summary\": \"Contains go:generate directives pointing at ../../ebpf/*.c programs with bpf2go, ensuring clang builds BPF object files.\",\n          \"key_points\": [\n            \"Generated *_bpf.go files (e.g., agentselector_bpf.go) expose typed structs (AgentSelectorObjects, Maps, Programs) and embed corresponding *.o bytecode, which internal/server/ebpf.go loads with pinned map support.\"\n          ]\n        }\n      ],\n      \"cross_dependencies\": [\n        \"scripts/build_ebpf.sh must run before any policy other than 'default' is used, otherwise loadEBPF will fail due to missing object files.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    \"scripts\": {\n      \"build_ebpf.sh\": {\n        \"role\": \"Rebuild internal/ebpfutil assets (`go generate ./internal/ebpfutil`) with clang, unless SKIP_EBPF_BUILD=true.\"\n      },\n      \"cleanup_ebpf_maps.sh\": {\n        \"role\": \"Remove pinned reuseport maps under $BPF_PIN_BASE to avoid stale file descriptor slots; skipped when BPF_PIN_PATH is set.\"\n      },\n      \"launch_server.sh\": {\n        \"role\": \"Build/run cmd/pebble_server with provided flags, enforce single instance via PID files, and optionally wrap in taskset.\"\n      },\n      \"dispatch_docker_experiments.py\": {\n        \"role\": \"NUMA-aware docker dispatcher: builds `docker run` commands per (policy, rate), streams logs, writes dispatch manifests, and can trigger run_exp.py on completion.\"\n      }\n    },\n    \"workload\": {\n      \"run_client.sh\": {\n        \"role\": \"Simplified wrapper to run cmd/workload_client without building binaries.\",\n        \"lines\": \"workload/run_client.sh:1-62\",\n        \"notes\": [\n          \"Accepts basic knobs (-s server, -t duration, -r rate, -g GET fraction, -m scan limit, -k prefix, -N key space, -w workers, -l log file).\",\n          \"Ensures client log directory exists, runs from repo root so go modules resolve, and writes logs to logs/client.log by default.\"\n        ]\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n"}
codex_stderr:1336:{"eval_agent_prompt": "You are an expert evaluator of high‑performance networking systems, specializing in Linux UDP servers that use SO_REUSEPORT with an eBPF sk_reuseport program to steer requests to worker sockets, backed by a database. There are quick point GET requests and longer scans. Your goal is to explain how the system actually behaved and why, using the provided artifacts: the current eBPF program, run metrics, and the previous program and its results. You will also have access to the entire codebase (in the current working directory). Included below is a codebase summary; read all the files and code you think necessarry to understand, but don't excessively read files and waste tokens. Use the prior run as a baseline for context, but do not limit analysis to simply deltas—surface deeper insights and analysis across all layers including the network, userspace, and database layers.\n\n**Method & guardrails**\n\n- Be precise but efficient and quick. Do not take too long running or waste tokens.\n- **Evidence only. No leaps.** If a claim is not supported by the artifacts, mark it **uncertain** and state exactly what’s missing. Be explicit about assumptions you cannot verify.\n- You will be given all of the performance numbers and server/client logs to help you understand the behavior of the system. You should use these as well as the codebase itself to confirm your hypothesis. \n- **You may only read codebase files in the following allowed directories and files: internal/, cmd/, scripts/, workload/, run_exp.py, run.sh\n- **Understand the codebase well and find specific lines and functions to support claims but don’t waste tokens.**\n- **Attribution.** Tie each observed effect to a plausible component (e.g. BPF routing, socket queues, userspace, DB) with specific evidence. If multiple hypotheses exist, list them with confidence levels.\n- Assume that there are no failures or stale entries in the reuseport map. \n\n**Output format (exactly three sections, concise but thorough):**\n\n1. **Behavior & performance (precise). Be concise.** \n    - Explain the performance of the system and why it got the performance metrics it did. Compare it to the baseline, when helpful, but surface deeper insights. Include Short, evidence‑backed explanations of *why*\n    - Include a confidence note (e.g., **High/Medium/Low**)\n2. **Did the behavior achieve the program’s intent? Why or why not?**\n    - Briefly restate the intent inferred from the eBPF program policy (e.g., “maintain per‑core locality/fairness”, “evening distribute requests among workers”).\n    - Judge success/failure of the behavior of this system to achieve the program intent, citing the specific metrics and evidence supporting each judgement.\n3. **Missing signals & new metrics/logs to add**\n    - Keep this list focused and actionable and at most a couple of items. Also consider how difficult it would be to implement it.  \n    - List the additional signals/metrics/logs that would close uncertainties, each with **what**, **how to collect**, and **why it helps**. Examples:\n        - Per‑socket backlog depth and time‑in‑queue (export via app counters every N seconds).\n        - Per‑RX‑queue and per‑core packet rates; softirq time; socket map hit/miss counts.\n\nBelow is included all of the relevant information.\n", "code": "/*\nPebble UDP reuseport selector — fast, verifier-friendly, congestion-resistant\n\nApproach\n- Remove the 128-iteration fully-unrolled probe loop. With a correct active\n  count, the first computed slot is valid; extra probes waste cycles and\n  inflate tail latency under load.\n- Compute the target slot without division:\n  * If active is a power of two: slot = hash & (active - 1).\n  * Otherwise: slot = ((hash * active) >> 32). This yields a near-uniform map\n    into [0, active) using a 64-bit multiply-high, avoiding slow u32 division\n    in eBPF.\n- Clamp active to the map's capacity (128) for safety.\n- Make exactly one bpf