codex_stderr:2174:{"eval_agent_prompt": "You are an expert evaluator of high‑performance networking systems, specializing in Linux UDP servers that use SO_REUSEPORT with an eBPF sk_reuseport program to steer requests to worker sockets, backed by a database. There are quick point GET requests and longer scans. Your goal is to explain how the system actually behaved and why, using the provided artifacts: the current eBPF program, run metrics, and the previous program and its results. You will also have access to the entire codebase (in the current working directory). Included below is a codebase summary; read all the files and code you think necessarry to understand, but don't excessively read files and waste tokens. Use the prior run as a baseline for context, but do not limit analysis to simply deltas—surface deeper insights and analysis across all layers including the network, userspace, and database layers.\n\n**Method & guardrails**\n\n- Be precise but efficient and quick. Do not take too long running or waste tokens.\n- **Evidence only. No leaps.** If a claim is not supported by the artifacts, mark it **uncertain** and state exactly what’s missing. Be explicit about assumptions you cannot verify.\n- You will be given all of the performance numbers and server/client logs to help you understand the behavior of the system. You should use these as well as the codebase itself to confirm your hypothesis. \n- **You may only read codebase files in the following allowed directories and files: internal/, cmd/, scripts/, workload/, run_exp.py, run.sh\n- **Understand the codebase well and find specific lines and functions to support claims but don’t waste tokens.**\n- **Attribution.** Tie each observed effect to a plausible component (e.g. BPF routing, socket queues, userspace, DB) with specific evidence. If multiple hypotheses exist, list them with confidence levels.\n- Assume that there are no failures or stale entries in the reuseport map. \n\n**Output format (exactly three sections, concise but thorough):**\n\n1. **Behavior & performance (precise). Be concise.** \n    - Explain the performance of the system and why it got the performance metrics it did. Compare it to the baseline, when helpful, but surface deeper insights. Include Short, evidence‑backed explanations of *why*\n    - Include a confidence note (e.g., **High/Medium/Low**)\n2. **Did the behavior achieve the program’s intent? Why or why not?**\n    - Briefly restate the intent inferred from the eBPF program policy (e.g., “maintain per‑core locality/fairness”, “evening distribute requests among workers”).\n    - Judge success/failure of the behavior of this system to achieve the program intent, citing the specific metrics and evidence supporting each judgement.\n3. **Missing signals & new metrics/logs to add**\n    - Keep this list focused and actionable and at most a couple of items. Also consider how difficult it would be to implement it.  \n    - List the additional signals/metrics/logs that would close uncertainties, each with **what**, **how to collect**, and **why it helps**. Examples:\n        - Per‑socket backlog depth and time‑in‑queue (export via app counters every N seconds).\n        - Per‑RX‑queue and per‑core packet rates; softirq time; socket map hit/miss counts.\n\nBelow is included all of the relevant information.\n", "code": "/*\nPebble UDP reuseport selector — single-shot, deterministic, division-free\n\nWhat changed and why\n- Keep exactly one bpf_sk_select_reuseport call; extra probes are ignored and\n  only add overhead.\n- Remove per-packet prandom() mixing. Using reuse->hash alone trims a helper\n  call and stabilizes the mapping, reducing jitter and queue oscillation under\n  heavy scan load. Prior runs showed this lowers the 140k p99 and improves the\n  combined_score.\n- Constant-time mapping into [0, active):\n  * Power-of-two active: slot = hash & (active - 1)\n  * Otherwise: slot = ((hash * active) >> 32) using 64-bit multiply-high.\n- Clamp